arm skin also plays a role—gasoline evaporates instantly on your warm skin, creating an intense localized cooling effect. That's a major factor: when you dip your finger in gasoline, the thin film evaporates extremely fast, making it feel icy. Water doesn’t evaporate nearly as fast on skin. So the primary reason is **evaporation rate**: gasoline’s high vapor pressure means it evaporates quickly, both in the container (cooling the bulk) and especially upon skin contact (enhancing the cold sensation). For a liquid like mercury, which hardly evaporates, it would feel cold only due to its high thermal conductivity, but not as dramatically cold as a fast-evaporating solvent. In summary, it’s the powerful self-refrigerating effect of evaporation that makes gasoline and similar volatile liquids seem colder, even when they start at the same room temperature.
